A. PROBLEM STATEMENT

Design Low-Level Design for Chess Game.

Requirements:

Support standard 8x8 chess board.
Support all standard chess pieces (King, Queen, Rook, Bishop, Knight, Pawn).
Players take alternate turns (White starts first).
Validate legal moves according to chess rules (including special moves: castling, en passant, promotion).
Detect check, checkmate, and stalemate.
Support basic undo (optional for LLD).

Constraints:

In-memory implementation.
Focus on core move validation and game state.

Edge Cases:

Invalid move.
Piece blocked by own piece.
King in check.
Pawn promotion.
Castling conditions.

B. HAPPY PATH STORY

=> Two players start a new game. White moves first and plays e4.
=> Black responds with e5.
=> White develops the Knight to f3.
=> The game continues with normal moves.
=> Later, White puts Black’s King in check. Black moves the King to safety.
=> Finally, White delivers checkmate, and the game ends with White winning.
=> This covers: Board setup, piece movement, turn management, and win condition.

C. 6 MANDATORY STEPS FOR LLD DESIGN

1. Clarify requirements

=> Functional: Initialize board, make moves, validate rules, detect check/checkmate.
=> Non-Functional: Efficient move validation.
=> Assumptions: 2 players, standard rules, no AI (human vs human).
=> Edge Cases: Castling, en passant, pawn promotion, pinned pieces.

2. Identify entities and relationships

=> Main entities : ChessGame, Board, Square, Piece, Player
=> Key relationships :
Board has 64 Squares.
Square can hold one Piece.
Piece belongs to one Player (color).

3. Design classes and method signatures

4. Create class diagrams

5. Explain LLD diagram verbally

=> ChessGame is the main class that holds the Board and current turn
=> Each Piece has its own isValidMove() method (polymorphism)
=> The Board validates if the path is clear and if the move puts the King in check
=> This design makes adding new piece rules easy

6. Discuss trade-offs and alternatives

=> Piece-specific validation vs single validator: Used polymorphism for cleaner code
=> 2D array for board vs Map: 2D array is faster for chess.
=> Can extend with undo using Memento pattern or move history.

